WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:08.333
In this walkthrough we'll read your
positions, group them, and see the
portfolio summary, in the OpenTRMS Workbench.

2
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:17.900
Portfolio Positions is not a list of trades.
It is what those trades add up to — one row
per instrument you hold, aggregated across books.

3
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:30.567
The columns come in bands. Position covers quantity and
notional, Valuation covers what it is worth, and profit and
loss sits beside it, so related numbers stay together as you scroll.

4
00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:41.267
Book and currency narrow the view. Positions are
aggregated across every book you can see, so
filtering to one book is how you get to a single desk's exposure.

5
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:51.200
Group by changes the shape of the answer.
Grouping by currency collapses the holdings into
one parent row per currency, with the count beside it.

6
00:00:51.200 --> 00:01:02.033
The same control groups by asset class, issuer, or
maturity bucket instead — the last of these being
how you see what is rolling off in the near term.

7
00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:11.267
Compact tightens the rows when you want more of
them on screen at once, and a view you have set
up can be saved as a template to come back to.

8
00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:20.767
The KPI Summary answers the same question one
level up. It takes the positions you just
filtered and reduces them to a card per currency.

9
00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:31.933
Each card carries market value, the day's profit
and loss, unrealized profit and loss, net
notional, and how many positions and deals sit behind it.

10
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:46.533
Because both surfaces share the same filters, the summary and the detail
always describe the same set of positions. That is the pattern across the
Workbench — work at the level you need, and drill down when a number looks wrong.

11
00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:55.400
That completes Positions and Portfolio KPIs. Every
surface shown here can also be opened from the agent
panel on the right, by asking for it in plain English.
